{"groups":[{"members":["c4","c1"],"primary":"c4","title":"California FEHA and Civil Rights Council regulations: limits on criminal-history screening","requirement":"Housing providers may not consider arrests that did not lead to a conviction, sealed or expunged records, or juvenile justice records, and may not use blanket bans on applicants with any criminal history. A denial based on a conviction must be directly related to the applicant's ability to be a good tenant and not a threat to the health and safety of others or the property, considering the nature and severity of the crime and the time elapsed.","key_value":"No blanket criminal-history bans; arrests without conviction, sealed or expunged records, and juvenile records may not be considered","status":"in_force","effective_date":"2020-01-01","end_date":null,"citation":"California Fair Employment and Housing Act (FEHA); California Civil Rights Council regulations (effective Jan. 1, 2020)","coverage_conditions":"Applies to most housing providers and tenant screening companies, including landlords, property managers, and third-party screeners, in most California housing accommodations.","exemptions":"When an owner occupies the home and rents to one additional person, the owner may exclude applicants based on protected characteristics, but still may not publish discriminatory statements or advertisements. Shared living areas in a single dwelling may be limited to one sex. Senior housing may be limited by age.","conflict_note":null},{"members":["c2"],"primary":"c2","title":"SB 329: source of income includes government housing subsidies (No Section 8 policies prohibited)","requirement":"SB 329 updated the FEHA definition of \"source of income\" to include government housing subsidy programs such as Section 8. Landlords may not refuse to participate in such programs, so \"No Section 8\" policies are unlawful.","key_value":"Refusing to participate in Section 8 or other government housing subsidy programs is prohibited","status":"in_force","effective_date":"2020-01-01","end_date":null,"citation":"SB 329 (Cal. 2019)","coverage_conditions":"Landlords in California; no building-type or size conditions are stated.","exemptions":null,"conflict_note":null},{"members":["c5","c3"],"primary":"c5","title":"Cal. Gov. Code § 12955 (as amended by SB 267): income-standard and credit-history limits for applicants with government rent subsidies","requirement":"When an applicant has a government rent subsidy, a landlord may not use a financial or income standard that is not based on the tenant's portion of the rent. A landlord using credit history must offer the option of lawful, verifiable alternative evidence of ability to pay, give reasonable time to respond, and reasonably consider that evidence instead of credit history.","key_value":"With a government rent subsidy, income standards must be based on the tenant's portion of rent and credit history requires an alternative-evidence option","status":"in_force","effective_date":"2024-01-01","end_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Gov. Code § 12955 (as amended by SB 267)","coverage_conditions":"Applies to owners of housing accommodations in California. The credit-history and income-standard provisions apply only where the applicant has a government rent subsidy (e.g., Section 8, HUD-VASH). Landlords may still ask about the level or source of income, verify employment, request landlord references and verify identity.","exemptions":null,"conflict_note":null}],"dropped":[]}
